Actually, I'd pick (d) GS principles may have something to do with
Eric's intelligence, but I don't necessarily understand the
relationship fully.  Please don't take my original response as a
rejection of your position, it just seemed to me to be incomplete. (As
it turns out, I was right. The description was incomplete as you said
below.) An old mental habit of mine, which I learned from talmudic
studies and I try to apply when I can, is that when you encounter
something you don't understand, you should say, "I don't understand,"
not "that's stupid."

BTW, I do recall that we discussed GS when we met at WorldCon.

> This bad habit is endemic in the non-GS-trained.  Also, I happen to know
> that you are an Objectivist.  Objectivism's epistemology reinforces this
> bad habit --- in fact, Rand's version is is actually *founded* on this
> particular map-territory error (though David Kelley's version may not be;
> I haven't read his stuff yet).  I have explained this in detail in
> previous postings.

I used to be an Objectivist, now I'm not sure I'm even an objectivist.
I've departed from Rand on several key concepts. In particular, I've
come to the conclusion that her formulation of Natural Rights -- such
as it is -- is unsupportable. I don't think humans have rights qua
humans. Instead, I think we've had a "fairness" ESS for so long that
we've started to think it's part of the basic package, so to speak.

I'm not sure how much of the Objectivist metaphysics I'd agree with. I
certainly do agree that it is pointless, at least, to wonder about
unfalsifiable metaphysical conditions (e.g. "what if I'm really a brain
in a vat somewhere with all of my experience piped in?") But I find it
extremely doubtful that anyone could create a formulation that would
tell you whether a particular symphony were "good" or "evil."

In fact, my support for Capitalism has reverted to a selfish (and
perhaps disturbingly Socially-Darwinian) premise. Capitalism supports
and enhances me and people like me. In general, it punishes people and
behaviour I don't like. Therefore, because I want the world to be
populated with me and mine to the exclusion of those who would wrong me
and mine, I support Capitalism.

> The lesson of GS is that, in any domain of discourse outside of a formal
> mathematical system, *you have always missed some of the facts*.  That
> is not a contingent, avoidable problem; it is inherent in the process of
> observation and abstraction from reality into language, *and it is why
> Aristotelian logic is insufficient*!

THIS is the key concept. Now all of the things you said fall into
place.  If I understand you correctly, then, outside the world of
mathematics, when a person says "Proposition P is true," they really
mean (or should really mean) "To the best of our ability, and
considering all of the data to which we have access at this time and
place, the proposition P seems to have the greatest predictive power
with regard to the system in question." That's an awful lot of
caveats. Still, it makes sense, given what we know from QM, Chaos, etc.
You can't know the physical state of a process, thus any statements
about it are inherently approximate.

Is that more what you had in mind? If so, I would agree entirely (and add
the GS texts mentioned to my ever-growing list of reading material).

The next question, and one that I know we discussed at WorldCon, is
why, then, do formal systems so often come up with models that have
such amazing predictive power?

