From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL/BOOK: Men Are Not Cost Effective

"Men Are Not Cost Effective" is the title of a recent book by June
Sephenson which argues that, because men far more crime (burglary 91%,
robbery 91%, murder 90%, drunk driving 87%, arson 87%, etc.), men
should be taxed ~$100/yr more as a criminal justice system usage fee.  

This is just the sort of proposal to make many folks blood boil.  But
if you want to have some fun, or get on the good side of a politically
correct thinker, trying talking about how this all makes sense to you.

You see, a private law system should do something similar.  If a
protection agency suffers extra costs to enforce law, costs not fully
obtained via restitution/compensation from criminals, then the
protection agencies should price their services to reflect the chance
they estimate that a customer will induce such costs.  Groups of
people inducing lower average extra costs should be offered lower
rates, just like with insurance.

Washington Times had an article on it 5/31/92, and the San Jose
Mercury News just did 4/17/93 (in front section).  

Robin Hanson

