Date: Mon, 19 Apr 1993 14:12:27 -0600
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: CRYPTO: The right to encrypt

>I would like to see T.O. Morrow show why people haven't successfully  
>used these same arguments in court against the current spate of civil  
>forfeitures taking place under the auspices of the "War on Drugs".  I  
>don't see any difference here, and certainly many of those arguments  
>he made speak DIRECTLY toward that issue and only tangentially toward  
>the encryption issue.
>
>Marina Vogel

By "these same arguments" I assume you are referring to my quick run
through the Bill of Rights.  None of the arguments relating to freedom of
speech, assembly, or privacy apply very directly to the WoD seizure cases. 
Nor do those cases help us to figure out whether or not intellecual
property can qualify for protection under the 5th Amendment's taking
clause.

I would say that asset seizure policies only tell us about how little
regard feds have for property rights delineated in the 4th and 5th
Amendments.  I agree that the precedents are not encouraging.  But note
that the Supreme Court has very recently held that those who are subjected
to asset seizures have a constitutional right to contest such takings in a
court of law.  As regards takings, the Court has also recently decided that
private property taken via regulation may require that compensation be
offered.  Perhaps, then, the pendulum has started to swing towards respect
for property rights.

But no matter which way the WoD asset seizure cases run, I don't thing they
will have much of an impact on our rights to cryptography.  I included
property-based rights arguments only as a fall-back.  I think that we'll
have to make privacy rights and 1st Amendment rights to speech and assembly
carry most of the legal weight.

