Subject: Korzybski's "E-Prime", also NLP

Elias Israel asks (asks Eric, but I assume I can play too):

> The next question, and one that I know we discussed at WorldCon, is
> why, then, do formal systems so often come up with models that have
> such amazing predictive power?

Ah yes, "the unreasonable utility of mathematics".

There's the Platonic view: The universe is constructed (somehow)
from mathematical principles.  We then discover these.

Another view (explained in _The Mathematical Experience_ by Davis
and Hersh, and doubtless lots of other places) is the "mathematics
by fiat" view -- the few things that actually are modelled well
with mathematics (physics or direct descendents of physics -- and
even here, they are often approximate at best) have made us so
delighted with mathematics that we deliberately force things into
the mathematical mold.  If it works (more or less), cool.  If not,
try again.  Given the expressiveness of mathematics, is it really
surprising that "more or less" accurate descriptions can be found
for lots of things?

They give some "word problems" about addition to exemplify the
problems -- many people know how to add  but do not know when
to add.  I provide some of these here because they're interesting.

Problem 1:
  One can of tuna fish costs $1.05.  How much does two cans of
  tuna fish cost?

Problem 2:
  A billion barrels of oil costs x dollars.  How much does a
  trillion barrels of oil cost?

Problem 3:
  A bank in computing a credit rating allows two points if you
  own a house, adds one point if your salary is over $20,000,
  adds one point if you have not moved in the last five years,
  subtracts one point if you have a criminal record, subtracts
  one point if you are under 25, etc.  What does the sum mean?
  [point: similar schemes are used all the time]

Problem 5:
  A cup of milk is added to a cup of popcorn.  How many cups of
  the mixture will result?

I haven't yet had time to read Korzybski (though it's been on
the pile for a couple of weeks), but I mention this issue
because it seems similar to what Eric was talking about:

  Many people know how to add but not when to add
  ###
  Many people know how to apply the law of the excluded middle
  but not when to apply it.

[no wise cracks like:  many people know how to use parallel
 constructions but not when to use them]

derek
